#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120776} :activity/id #uuid "e4df6ef2-6323-5da7-9712-42a8b0ec2db6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051049 :topic/id #uuid "7d21c9de-358a-52ec-b910-f6117a085d7f" :topic/math-academy-id 1814} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120993 :tutorial/content "Until now, all limits of the form $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ that we have encountered have evaluated to either a finite number or $\\text{DNE}$. However, there is another possibility: the limit could be *infinite*!\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. This function has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$, and the function value $f(x)$ grows without bound as $x$ approaches the asymptote $x=1$. What is the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x)$?\n\n![](images/ab/ab9d57d3952fe0221616f9e64e1295fd0511703bdd5ed3a5fe91113cc7c8bf1b.png)\n\nRemember that in order for computing any limit, in this case, $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x)$, we have to compute the left and right-sided limits separately and ensure that they match up.\n\n- **Left-sided limit:** As we approach $x=1$ (the vertical asymptote) from the left side of the graph, the function $f(x)$ increases without bound, approaching $\\infty$. We conclude that\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\infty$.\n- **Right-sided limit:** Likewise, as we approach the vertical asymptote $x=1$ from the right side of the graph, $f(x)$ still approaches $\\infty$. Consequently,\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^+} f(x) = \\infty$.\n\nWe see that both the left and right limits exist and are equal to $\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^+} f(x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nAs a result, the overall limit is also equal to $\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x) = \\infty\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6db8e507-0d4f-555c-b39b-e6a52ace2cd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 156 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8d9bad47-bf42-52f0-a4a8-b5dbad51af8c" :step/math-academy-id 294 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119869 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119528 :question/id #uuid "975987da-103c-5f24-93f3-56991b9e8b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-14"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba9f608-c7bc-5eb6-ae4e-5f62cb7222a7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120392}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are Different"} :step/id #uuid "35c7e21e-4a92-5131-8faf-6c68f8f3b4ef" :step/math-academy-id 295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119535 :question/id #uuid "a9e611ae-68f1-5618-a53b-2c9b3606d468" :question/math-academy-id "e-15"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e021f949-0f55-50b8-8681-0b1ce71b21d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120393}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are the Same"} :step/id #uuid "7080f382-f1a7-53d8-b3be-36c1ef781e75" :step/math-academy-id 299 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120777}}] :activity/title "Infinite Limits from Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120022}]}
